WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Each outcome so far concerned one protein copy.

2
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.360
But a cell controls a whole population.

3
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.540
If a kinase writes the mark, why cannot kinase activity alone predict the level?

4
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.700
Follow a fixed population while writing and erasing happen together.

5
00:00:21.183 --> 00:00:26.463
Divide the population into an unphosphorylated pool and a phosphorylated pool.

6
00:00:27.683 --> 00:00:31.683
The kinase moves available copies toward the phosphorylated side.

7
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:35.223
The phosphatase moves marked copies back.

8
00:00:36.423 --> 00:00:41.003
The total number of protein copies stays fixed while membership keeps changing.

9
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:47.587
Use the simplest model: one site, a fixed pool, and effective first-order rates.

10
00:00:48.947 --> 00:00:52.267
Kinase flow depends on how many unmarked copies remain.

11
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:57.207
Phosphatase flow depends on how many marked copies exist.

12
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:03.407
A steady level appears when those opposing flows match, although individual copies

13
00:01:03.407 --> 00:01:04.407
still cycle.

14
00:01:05.217 --> 00:01:06.397
Make a prediction.

15
00:01:07.797 --> 00:01:10.657
Keep kinase activity fixed but slow the phosphatase.

16
00:01:11.997 --> 00:01:14.597
Which way will the phosphorylated fraction move?

17
00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:21.973
The fraction rises.

18
00:01:23.693 --> 00:01:27.853
Writing now outruns erasing at the old level, so marked copies accumulate.

19
00:01:29.173 --> 00:01:32.193
As that pool grows, phosphatase flow also grows.

20
00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:36.673
A new steady level appears when the flows match again.

21
00:01:38.013 --> 00:01:41.593
Inhibiting an eraser can therefore mimic activating a writer.

22
00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:44.657
The balance has a compact rule.

23
00:01:45.897 --> 00:01:50.737
At steady state, the kinase rate divided by the sum of both rates gives the

24
00:01:50.737 --> 00:01:51.997
phosphorylated fraction.

25
00:01:53.357 --> 00:01:56.077
Compute that rule across possible kinase rates.

26
00:01:57.197 --> 00:02:02.057
The curve rises smoothly and approaches a ceiling rather than jumping at a fixed

27
00:02:02.057 --> 00:02:03.057
threshold.

28
00:02:03.717 --> 00:02:06.577
The same enzymes also set response speed.

29
00:02:07.897 --> 00:02:09.857
Increase both rates by the same factor.

30
00:02:11.057 --> 00:02:14.737
The steady fraction stays put because their ratio is unchanged.

31
00:02:16.077 --> 00:02:20.857
The population reaches that level faster because the opposing flows are larger.

32
00:02:22.457 --> 00:02:24.577
Faster cycling spends ATP faster.

33
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.350
A signal can act at either side of the cycle.

34
00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:34.630
It may activate the kinase, inhibit the phosphatase, or later activate the

35
00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.350
phosphatase to end the response.

36
00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.490
Measuring only the writer misses those possibilities.

37
00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.830
Measure the substrate's modification and both opposing activities.

38
00:02:46.983 --> 00:02:48.983
Keep the model's boundary visible.

39
00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:54.643
It assumes a fixed pool, a single site, and effective first-order rates.

40
00:02:56.043 --> 00:03:01.123
Saturated enzymes, several sites, feedback, or protein turnover can change the

41
00:03:01.123 --> 00:03:06.703
response. Start with the simple balance, then test whether the system obeys it.

42
00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:11.447
When phosphorylation rises, ask what changed on both sides.

43
00:03:12.707 --> 00:03:15.747
More kinase activity is only a possible answer.

44
00:03:17.147 --> 00:03:20.987
Less phosphatase activity can produce the same direction of change.

45
00:03:22.247 --> 00:03:26.387
The observed level belongs to the cycle, not to either enzyme alone.

46
00:03:27.647 --> 00:03:29.807
That is why the substrate measurement matters.
